📘 TOPIC 3: REGRESSION MODEL EVALUATION
1. Why Evaluate a Model?

Training a model isn't enough.

We need to know:

How good are its predictions?

Important regression metrics include:

MAE
MSE
RMSE
R²
Adjusted R²
2. MAE
MAE=1n∑∣yi−y^i∣\boxed{ MAE=\frac1n\sum |y_i-\hat y_i| }

MAE tells us the average absolute prediction error.

Lower MAE is generally better when comparing models on the same problem and scale.

3. MSE
MSE=1n∑(yi−y^i)2\boxed{ MSE=\frac1n\sum(y_i-\hat y_i)^2 }

MSE squares errors.

Therefore, large prediction mistakes receive greater penalties.

4. RMSE
RMSE=MSE\boxed{RMSE=\sqrt{MSE}}

RMSE has an important advantage:

It returns the error to the same units as the target.

For example, if salary is measured in rupees, RMSE is also expressed in rupees.

5. MAE vs MSE vs RMSE
Metric	Error used	UnitsMAE	Absolute	Original
MSE	Squared	Squared
RMSE	Squared then square root	Original
6. R² Score

R² is called the Coefficient of Determination.

Formula:

R2=1−SSESST\boxed{ R^2=1-\frac{SSE}{SST} }
7. Understanding SST

SST = Total Sum of Squares

SST=∑(yi−yˉ)2\boxed{ SST=\sum(y_i-\bar y)^2 }

It measures total squared variation of actual target values around their mean.

8. Understanding SSE

SSE = Sum of Squared Errors

SSE=∑(yi−y^i)2\boxed{ SSE=\sum(y_i-\hat y_i)^2 }

It measures the model's total squared prediction error.

9. R² Example

Suppose:

SST=1000SST=1000

and:

SSE=200SSE=200

Then:

R2=1−2001000R^2=1-\frac{200}{1000} R2=1−0.2R^2=1-0.2 R2=0.8\boxed{R^2=0.8}

Interpretation:

The model achieves an 80% reduction in squared prediction error compared with predicting the target mean.

10. Understanding R² Values
R²	Meaning1	Perfect predictions
0.9	90% reduction in squared error relative to mean baseline
0.5	50% reduction
0	Same squared error as mean baseline
Negative	Worse than mean baseline
Important
R2=0.90R^2=0.90

does NOT mean:

Model has 90% accuracy.

R² is not a classification accuracy percentage.

Your handwritten notes cover R² and Adjusted R² on the performance-metrics section.

Important Correction to Remember

Although the handwritten note says R² is always positive, R² can actually be negative.

A negative R² indicates that the model's squared-error performance is worse than simply predicting the mean.

11. Problem With Ordinary R²

Suppose:

Model	Features	Training R²A	2	0.85
B	10	0.86

Model B uses eight additional features for only a small increase in training R².

Does that automatically mean Model B is better?

No.

In nested ordinary least-squares models with an intercept evaluated on the same training observations, adding predictors cannot decrease training R².

12. Adjusted R²

Adjusted R² adds a predictor-count penalty.

Radj2=1−(1−R2)n−1n−p−1\boxed{ R^2_{adj} = 1-(1-R^2) \frac{n-1}{n-p-1} }

Where:

nn = number of observations
pp = number of predictors excluding intercept
R2R^2 = ordinary R²

The standard formula requires:

n>p+1\boxed{n>p+1}
13. Adjusted R² Example

Suppose:

R2=0.90R^2=0.90 n=100n=100 p=4p=4

Then:

Radj2=1−(1−0.90)9995R^2_{adj} = 1-(1-0.90)\frac{99}{95} Radj2≈0.8958R^2_{adj}\approx0.8958 Radj2≈0.8958\boxed{R^2_{adj}\approx0.8958}
14. R² vs Adjusted R²
R²	Adjusted R²Measures fit	Measures fit with predictor-count penalty
No feature-count penalty	Penalizes extra predictors
Cannot decrease when adding predictors in nested training OLS	Can decrease
Can be negative	Can be negative
Core Concept

Adjusted R² increases only when improvement in training fit is sufficient to overcome the complexity penalty.

But remember:

Good R² or Adjusted R² does not automatically guarantee good performance on unseen data.

