📘 TOPIC 4: OVERFITTING, UNDERFITTING, GENERALIZATION, BIAS & VARIANCE
1. The Real Goal of Machine Learning

The goal isn't simply:

Perform extremely well on training data.

The actual goal is:

Learn patterns that generalize to unseen data\boxed{ \text{Learn patterns that generalize to unseen data} }
2. Underfitting

Underfitting occurs when the model fails to capture important patterns in the data.

Typical pattern:

Poor Training Performance+Poor Test Performance\boxed{ Poor\ Training\ Performance + Poor\ Test\ Performance }
Exam Analogy

Imagine a student studies only a few basic formulas.

Result:

Poor practice performance
Poor examination performance

The student hasn't learned enough.

3. Overfitting

Overfitting happens when the model fits training-specific details too closely and performs poorly on new data.

Typical pattern:

Excellent Training Performance+Much Worse Test Performance\boxed{ Excellent\ Training\ Performance + Much\ Worse\ Test\ Performance }
Exam Analogy

A student memorizes practice questions without understanding the concepts.

Result:

Excellent practice score
Poor performance on unfamiliar exam questions
4. Good Fitting

A good model learns useful patterns that transfer to new observations.

Typical pattern:

Good Training Performance+Good Test Performance\boxed{ Good\ Training\ Performance + Good\ Test\ Performance }
Example
Model	Training R²	Test R²	InterpretationA	0.98	0.55	Likely overfitting
B	0.45	0.43	Possible underfitting
C	0.92	0.90	Good generalization

These are illustrative patterns, not universal thresholds.

Your notes contain the same idea by comparing training and testing performance for overfitting, good fitting and underfitting.

5. Bias

Bias represents systematic error associated with assumptions made by the learning method.

High Bias

A model is too restrictive to capture the important pattern.

High bias is often associated with:

Underfitting\boxed{Underfitting}
Example

Suppose the true relationship is strongly curved.

You try fitting only a simple straight line.

The model may not be flexible enough.

6. Variance

Variance measures how sensitive the learned model is to changes in the training dataset.

High Variance

The model changes substantially when trained on different samples.

High variance is commonly associated with:

Overfitting\boxed{Overfitting}
7. Bias-Variance Tradeoff
Complexity	Bias	Variance	RiskToo low	High	Often low	Underfitting
Suitable	Balanced	Manageable	Better generalization
Too high	Often low	High	Overfitting

For squared-error regression:

Expected Error=Bias2+Variance+Irreducible Noise\boxed{ Expected\ Error = Bias^2+Variance+Irreducible\ Noise }

Prediction error can therefore arise from:

Systematic model limitations
Sensitivity to training data
Unavoidable noise
Most Important Lesson

Our goal is not simply minimizing bias.

Our goal is not simply minimizing variance.

Our goal is:

Minimize expected prediction error\boxed{\text{Minimize expected prediction error}}
8. Fixing Underfitting

Possible approaches:

Use a more flexible model.
Add useful features.
Reduce excessive regularization.
Improve feature engineering.
Make sure optimization has converged.

Simple memory:

Underfitting → Consider increasing useful flexibility.

9. Fixing Overfitting

Possible approaches:

Reduce unnecessary model complexity.
Apply regularization.
Remove irrelevant features.
Collect more representative training data.
Use cross-validation.
Prevent data leakage.

Simple memory:

Overfitting → Control unnecessary flexibility.

10. Training vs Validation/Test Performance

Training performance tells us:

How well did the model fit data used during learning?

Validation/test performance tells us:

How well does the trained model perform on unseen data?

That's why:

High Training R2≠Guaranteed Good Model\boxed{ High\ Training\ R^2 \neq Guaranteed\ Good\ Model }

Generalization matters more than memorizing the training dataset.

