📘 TOPIC 5: REGULARIZATION, RIDGE, LASSO, LAMBDA & FEATURE SCALING
1. What Is Regularization?

Regularization is used to control model complexity by adding a penalty to the training objective.

Without regularization:

Minimize Prediction Error\boxed{\text{Minimize Prediction Error}}

With regularization:

Minimize Prediction Error + Penalty\boxed{ \text{Minimize Prediction Error + Penalty} }

The penalty discourages unnecessarily large coefficients.

This is especially useful when trying to control model complexity and reduce overfitting.

Your handwritten notes introduce Ridge and Lasso after the discussion of overfitting and underfitting.

2. Ridge Regression

Ridge uses:

L2 Regularization\boxed{L2\ Regularization}

A common objective is:

JRidge=12n∑(yi−y^i)2+λ∑j=1pθj2\boxed{ J_{Ridge} = \frac{1}{2n} \sum(y_i-\hat y_i)^2 + \lambda \sum_{j=1}^{p}\theta_j^2 }

Ridge penalizes the squares of coefficients.

3. Ridge Penalty Example

Suppose:

θ1=3\theta_1=3 θ2=−4\theta_2=-4

Then the unscaled L2 penalty is:

32+(−4)23^2+(-4)^2 =9+16=9+16 L2=25\boxed{L2=25}

The actual regularization term includes λ\lambda:

λ(25)\lambda(25)
4. What Does Ridge Do?

Ridge generally:

Shrinks coefficients toward zero
Does not normally make them exactly zero
Can stabilize coefficients
Is useful when predictors are correlated
Controls complexity without automatically eliminating features
Memory Trick

Ridge = Reduce coefficients

5. Lasso Regression

Lasso uses:

L1 Regularization\boxed{L1\ Regularization}

A common objective is:

JLasso=12n∑(yi−y^i)2+λ∑j=1p∣θj∣\boxed{ J_{Lasso} = \frac{1}{2n} \sum(y_i-\hat y_i)^2 + \lambda \sum_{j=1}^{p}|\theta_j| }

Lasso penalizes the absolute values of coefficients.

6. Lasso Penalty Example

Suppose:

θ1=3\theta_1=3 θ2=−4\theta_2=-4

Then:

∣3∣+∣−4∣|3|+|-4| =3+4=3+4 L1=7\boxed{L1=7}

Again, this is the unscaled penalty.

The regularization term includes:

λ(7)\lambda(7)
7. Lasso and Feature Selection

An important property of Lasso is that it can make some coefficients exactly zero.

Suppose originally:

y^=10+5x1+3x2+2x3\hat y = 10+5x_1+3x_2+2x_3

After Lasso, an illustrative result might be:

y^=10+4.5x1+2.8x2+0x3\hat y = 10+4.5x_1+2.8x_2+0x_3

Therefore:

y^=10+4.5x1+2.8x2\boxed{ \hat y=10+4.5x_1+2.8x_2 }

Feature x3x_3 no longer contributes to the prediction.

Therefore, Lasso can perform automatic feature selection.

Memory Trick

Lasso = Reduce coefficients and Leave some Out

8. Ridge vs Lasso
